h】\nL41:   * 【4†HD Clinicians and Researchers】\nL42:     * 【4†Clinicians & Researchers Home】\nL43:     * 【28†HD Science Think Tank】\nL44:     * 【7†EHDN Working Groups】\nL45:     * 【11†Fellowship programme】\nL46:     * 【9†Seed Fund Applications】\nL47:     * 【12†Grant management & Collaborations】\nL48:     * 【10†Data-Mining Applications】\nL49:     * 【8†Rater training and certification】\nL50:     * 【6†Research & Publications】\nL51:     * 【29†Guidelines】\nL52:   * 【13†Clinical Trial Sponsors】\nL53:     * 【13†Clinical Trial Sponsors Home】\nL54:     * 【30†Protocol Advice】\nL55:     * 【14†Feasibility Assessment】\nL56:     * 【16†Operational Support】\nL57:     * 【17†Recruitment】\nL58:     * 【18†Clinical Rater Training】\nL59:     * 【19†Site Certification Scheme】\nL60: ## Discontinuation of GENERATION HD2 (tominersen) and POINT-HD (RG6496)\nL61: \nL62: Posted on 【31†July 10, 2026】 by 【32†Lisanne Walter】\nL63: \nL64: On Thursday 9 July 2026, Roche announced the discontinuation of two EHDN-endorsed studies:\nL65: • GENERATION HD2 (【33†clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT05686551†clinicaltrials.gov】): A phase 2/open-label extension study of tominersen compared with placebo in Huntington’s disease (HD).\nL66: • POINT-HD (【34†clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT07246941†clinicaltrials.gov】): A phase 1 study of RG6496 delivered intrathecally compared with placebo in HD.\nL67: \nL68: The community letter from Roche can be read in full 【35†here】.\nL69: We share the disappointment of the HD community and will shortly issue a full statement from the EHDN Executive Committee via our usual channels.\nL70: \nL71: We remain grateful for the unwavering generosity and commitment of the HD community to taking part in research and, in doing so, advancing our understanding of HD.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRoche/Genentech Discontinuing Trials – International Huntington Association (https://huntington-disease.org/2026/07/09/roche-genentech-discontinuing-trials/)\nciteturn96view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://huntington-disease.org/2026/07/09/roche-genentech-discontinuing-trials/\",\"lineno\":118}); Total lines: 166\nL0: 【0†Skip to content】\nL1:   * 【1†English】\nL2:   * 【2†Français】\nL3:   * 【3†العربية】\nL4:   * 【4†简体中文】\nL5:   * 【5†日本語】\nL6:   * 【6†Português】\nL7:   * 【7†Русский】\nL8:   * 【8†Español】\nL9:   * 【9†More Languages】\nL10:     * 【10†Български】\nL11:     * 【11†Čeština】\nL12:     * 【12†Dansk】\nL13:     * 【13†Nederlands】\nL14:     * 【14†Eesti】\nL15:     * 【15†Suomi】\nL16:     * 【16†Deutsch】\nL17:     * 【17†Ελληνικά】\nL18:     * 【18†Magyar】\nL19:     * 【19†Bahasa Indonesia】\nL20:     * 【20†Italiano】\nL21:     * 【21†Latviešu valoda】\nL22:     * 【22†Lietuvių kalba】\nL23:     * 【23†Polski】\nL24:     * 【24†Română】\nL25:     * 【25†Slovenčina】\nL26:     * 【26†Slovenščina】\nL27:     * 【27†Svenska】\nL28:     * 【28†Türkçe】\nL29:     * 【29†Українська】\nL30:     * 【30†كوردی‎】\nL31:   * 【1†English】\nL32:   * 【2†Français】\nL33:   * 【3†العربية】\nL34:   * 【4†简体中文】\nL35:   * 【5†日本語】\nL36:   * 【6†Português】\nL37:   * 【7†Русский】\nL38:   * 【8†Español】\nL39:   * 【9†More Languages】\nL40:     * 【10†Български】\nL41:     * 【11†Čeština】\nL42:     * 【12†Dansk】\nL43:     * 【13†Nederlands】\nL44:     * 【14†Eesti】\nL45:     * 【15†Suomi】\nL46:     * 【16†Deutsch】\nL47:     * 【17†Ελληνικά】\nL48:     * 【18†Magyar】\nL49:     * 【19†Bahasa Indonesia】\nL50:     * 【20†Italiano】\nL51:     * 【21†Latviešu valoda】\nL52:     * 【22†Lietuvių kalba】\nL53:     * 【23†Polski】\nL54:     * 【24†Română】\nL55:     * 【25†Slovenčina】\nL56:     * 【26†Slovenščina】\nL57:     * 【27†Svenska】\nL58:     * 【28†Türkçe】\nL59:     * 【29†Українська】\nL60:     * 【30†كوردی‎】\nL61:   * 【31†Home】\nL62:   * 【32†About Us】\nL63:     * 【33†Mission】\nL64:     * 【34†Board members】\nL65:     * 【35†Operational team】\nL66:   * 【36†Huntington´s Disease】\nL67:     * 【36†Huntington’s Disease】\nL68:     * 【37†Juvenile Huntingtons Disease (JHD)】\nL69:     * 【38†Predictive Genetic Test】\nL70:   * 【39†Associations】\nL71:     * 【39†Find Your National Organization】\nL72:     * 【40†Create an Association】\nL73:   * 【41†News】\nL74:   * 【42†Events】\nL75:     * 【42†Calendar】\nL76:     * 【43†Past Events】\nL77:   * 【44†Resources】\nL78:     * 【45†HD Blog】\nL79:     * 【46†HD Column】\nL80:     * 【47†HD Focus – News Around the Globe†news.huntington-disease.org】\nL81:     * 【48†Videos】\nL82:   * 【49†Advocacy】\nL83:     * 【49†Volunteer】\nL84:     * 【50†HD-CAB†www.hd-cab.org】\nL85:   * 【51†Contact Us】\nL86:   * 【31†Home】\nL87:   * 【32†About Us】\nL88:     * 【33†Mission】\nL89:     * 【34†Board members】\nL90:     * 【35†Operational team】\nL91:   * 【36†Huntington´s Disease】\nL92:     * 【36†Huntington’s Disease】\nL93:     * 【37†Juvenile Huntingtons Disease (JHD)】\nL94:     * 【38†Predictive Genetic Test】\nL95:   * 【39†Associations】\nL96:     * 【39†Find Your National Organization】\nL97:     * 【40†Create an Association】\nL98:   * 【41†News】\nL99:   * 【42†Events】\nL100:     * 【42†Calendar】\nL101:     * 【43†Past Events】\nL102:   * 【44†Resources】\nL103:     * 【45†HD Blog】\nL104:     * 【46†HD Column】\nL105:     * 【47†HD Focus – News Around the Globe†news.huntington-disease.org】\nL106:     * 【48†Videos】\nL107:   * 【49†Advocacy】\nL108:     * 【49†Volunteer】\nL109:     * 【50†HD-CAB†www.hd-cab.org】\nL110:   * 【51†Contact Us】\nL111: 【52†Donate】\nL112: \nL113: 【53†back to all†www.huntington-disease.org】\nL114: # Roche/Genentech Discontinuing Trials\nL115: \nL116:   * July 9, 2026\nL117: \nL118: Today Roche shared difficult news. After careful review of the data, they have decided to stop two of their Huntington’s disease studies: GENERATION HD2 and POINT-HD. We’ve provided an overview along with further details provided in a community letter from Roche to help explain these recent decisions.\nL119: ### In Summary:\nL120: GENERATION HD2 was testing tominersen, the second time this drug has been studied in a large trial. An earlier study, GENERATION HD1, was stopped a few years ago, and this study was designed to try again at a lower dose in people at an earlier stage of HD. The results showed tominersen was safe and did lower both the normal and harmful form of the huntingtin protein, along with a marker of damage in the brain called NfL.\nL121: Unfortunately, it did not slow the course of HD or improve how participants were doing, so the study is ending.\nL122: POINT-HD tested a newer drug, RG6496. It was designed to be more selective than tominersen, lowering mainly the harmful form of the huntingtin protein while sparing more of the normal form. It stopped early after animal data showed it could not be given safely long term. Everyone who received a dose is safe and will continue to be monitored.\nL123: ## More Details from Roche\nL124: \nL125: These are independent, data-driven events, which have coincided by chance. While this news is deeply disappointing, promptly communicating about the findings is the most responsible way for us to honor the contribution of study participants and allow the HD community to focus efforts on other avenues of research.\nL126: ### GENERATION HD2/tominersen: Study Results\nL127: \nL128: The GENERATION HD2 study was designed to validate a data trend found in adults with early or very subtle symptoms of HD after scientists analysed the results of a prior tominersen study (GENERATION HD1). Recently, the 16-month treatment period for all GENERATION HD2 participants was completed, data collected and initial analyses were conducted. Initial results of the primary analysis show:\nL129:   * Safety: Tominersen was well tolerated and showed no new safety signals.\nL130:   * Biomarkers: Tominersen significantly lowered mutant huntingtin protein and Neurofilament Light Chain (NfL) (a protein linked to neuronal damage that is elevated in people with HD), compared to people receiving placebo.\nL131:   * Efficacy: There was no meaningful impact on clinical efficacy for the study participants receiving tominersen, compared to those on placebo.\nL132: ### POINT-HD/RG6496: New Findings from Non-clinical (Animal) Studies\nL133: \nL134: The POINT-HD study is the first time RG6496 is being tested in people. The study’s main aims were evaluating the effects of a single dose of the drug in people with early HD. The study recently opened and has enrolled three participants.\nL135: In parallel to the POINT-HD study, Roche was conducting longer-term non-clinical (animal) research to support future plans to give multiple doses of the drug. Based on new data from one of these studies, our team has concluded that RG6496 cannot be given chronically with repeated doses. While there is no safety concern for people to receive one dose, we have chosen to stop the POINT-HD study early, because we can no longer offer participants the possibility of long-term treatment.\nL136: Enrolled participants will continue to be monitored, per study protocol.\nL137: ## Next Steps\nL138:   * Communications to Study Participants: Our immediate focus is supporting participants and sites regarding these announcements. Earlier this week, sites were notified so they could contact participants and discuss transition plans and support. Because these studies are running in multiple countries, we acknowledge that families may have received news at different times and in different channels. For further questions, families should contact their sites. Our team can also be reached at medinfo.roche.com.\nL139:   * Sharing Data & Learnings: Data will continue to be analysed and presented at future medical meetings. We are committed to sharing learnings with the research and family communities to advance the understanding of drug development for HD.\nL140:   * Further Research: Roche’s Phase I/II study of investigational gene therapy RG6662 (formerly from Spark Therapeutics; study SPK-101, NCT06826612) is ongoing as planned. Additionally our interest in exploring multiple HD therapeutic approaches remains, and we will continue to follow promising science.\nL141: ## With profound thanks to the HD community\nL142: > > We have been humbled and inspired by the 1,500+ HD families and broader community who contributed to both programmes – tominersen since clinical studies began in 2015 with our partner Ionis Pharmaceuticals, and RG6496 more recently. These contributions changed the history of HD drug development – proving the protein that causes HD could be lowered in humans, shaping new research and approaches, which will undoubtedly lead to future breakthroughs.\nL143: >>\nL144: >> On behalf of all Roche HD team members over the years, we are deeply grateful to this incredible HD community. Progress is only possible together and built over years of partnership. We remain hopeful for the future of HD research.\nL145: ### About the GENERATION HD2 Study\nL146: The GENERATION HD2 study (NCT05686551) is a Phase II study that evaluated the safety, biomarker impacts, and clinical efficacy trends of tominersen, compared to placebo over a minimum of 16 months. It investigated whether a 100mg dose of tominersen administered via spinal fluid injection three times a year could offer clinical benefit to adults aged 25-50 years with early HD.\nL147: In addition to safety and biomarker measures, tominersen was evaluated using composite Unified Huntington’s Disease Rating Scale (cUHDRS) or Total Functional Capacity (TFC) scores – scales that are commonly used in HD studies to understand the impact on disease progression. The study enrolled 301 participants across 15 countries (Argentina, Australia, Austria, Canada, Denmark, France, Germany, Italy, New Zealand, Poland, Portugal, Spain, Switzerland, the UK and the USA).\nL148: ### About the POINT-HD Study\n"
